## Who does what
- Do small things yourself: open a known file, run a test, make an edit. A helper costs more
  than it saves on small jobs.
- `explorer` (Haiku): only when you would otherwise read 5+ files or a long output.
- `planner` (Opus): a plan before multi-file features, refactors or unclear fixes.
- `designer` (Opus): builds a whole page people will see (site, landing page, UI) in one
  pass. Give it the request; don't build such pages yourself.
- `debugger`: a bug two fixes haven't solved. `researcher`: web or docs lookups.
- `reviewer` (Haiku): diffs over ~50 lines or touching security, data or money.
- `big-decision` (Fable, rare): costly-to-reverse choices.
- If a helper is refused permission, do that step yourself.

## Every change
- Only what was asked, in the fewest lines that fully solve it; reuse existing code first.
- Prove it with the narrowest test. New behaviour gets a test.
- Unfamiliar area: read `CODEMAP.md` first; `/map` at the end if files were added or moved.
- Replies: the answer, then at most one short follow-up.
- Same correction twice, or "always/never": add a rule to `.claude/LEARNED.md`.
- Never read or print secrets; never commit `.env`, keys or tokens.

